 false, "mention": "humans", "obj": "species", "prob": 0.9879711270332336, "span": {"begin": 706, "end": 712}}, {"id": ["mesh:D000728"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "androgen", "obj": "drug", "prob": 0.9992709755897522, "span": {"begin": 920, "end": 928}}, {"id": ["mesh:D000728"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "androgens", "obj": "drug", "prob": 0.9990249872207642, "span": {"begin": 1039, "end": 1048}}, {"id": ["mesh:D000728"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "androgen", "obj": "drug", "prob": 0.9996322393417358, "span": {"begin": 1219, "end": 1227}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:367"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "androgen receptor", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9861953854560852, "span": {"begin": 1219, "end": 1236}}, {"id": ["mesh:C078547"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "KE-2274", "obj": "drug", "prob": 0.9953593015670776, "span": {"begin": 1256, "end": 1263}}, {"id": ["mesh:D000728"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "androgen", "obj": "drug", "prob": 0.9976692795753479, "span": {"begin": 1317, "end": 1325}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:367"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "androgen receptor", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9971451759338379, "span": {"begin": 1317, "end": 1334}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:367", "231", "2232"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "AR", "obj": "gene", "prob": null, "span": {"begin": 1391, "end": 1393}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:367", "231", "2232"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "AR", "obj": "gene", "prob": null, "span": {"begin": 1521, "end": 1523}}, {"id": ["mesh:D013739"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "testosterone", "obj": "drug", "prob": 0.9549418091773987, "span": {"begin": 1563, "end": 1575}}, {"id": ["mesh:D013196"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "dihydrotestosterone", "obj": "drug", "prob": 0.9997898936271667, "span": {"begin": 1627, "end": 1646}}, {"id": ["mesh:D013196"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "DHT", "obj": "drug", "prob": 0.999244213104248, "span": {"begin": 1648, "end": 1651}}], "text": "This KER describes how increased androgen receptor (AR) activation in females during fetal development can lead to an increased AGD. The AR is a member of the steroid hormone receptor family and mediates the biological effects of androgens. Increased AR activation can occur either through AR agonism (i.e., activation of the receptor by a compound) or through elevated levels of endogenous hormones such as testosterone or dihydrotestosterone (DHT) (Dalton &amp; Gao, 2010; Davey &amp; Grossmann, 2016; Luetjens &amp; Weinbauer, 2012; Naamneh Elzenaty et al., 2022; Sutinen et al., 2017).   Anogenital distance (AGD) refers to the distance between the anus and the external genitalia. In both rodents and humans, female AGD is approximately half the length of male AGD (Liu et al., 2014; Salazar-Martinez et al, 2004; Schwartz et al., 2019; Sharpe, 2020; Thankamony at al., 2016; Wise, 2024). This difference is due to androgen-dependent regulation of perineal growth during fetal development in males, whereas in females, the absence of androgens limits this growth (Ipulan at al., 2016; Schwartz at al., 2019; Sharpe, 2020; Welsh at al., 2008).   It should be noted that the upstream Key Event (KE) &lsquo;increase, androgen receptor activation&rsquo; (KE-2274) specifically focuses on increased activation of the androgen receptor in vivo, while most methods that can be used to measure AR activity are carried out in vitro. Indirect information about this KE may for example be provided from assays showing in vitro AR agonism, increased in vitro or in vivo testosterone production/levels or increased in vitro or in vivo dihydrotestosterone (DHT) production/levels.", "timestamp": "Tue Oct 13 15:59:14 +0000 2026"}